  "prompt_prefix": "/deep-research ",
  "instruction": "According to the CDC, which US states had a death rate due to heart disease >191.5, a death rate due to cancer >161.9, and a death rate due to diabetes >33.1 in 2022?",
  "answer": "Based on the CDC 2022 age-adjusted death rate data (sourced from CDC WONDER via KFF State Health Facts), I've cross-referenced all three datasets. Here are the states meeting **all three thresholds**:\n\n| State | Heart Disease | Cancer | Diabetes |\n|-------|---------------|--------|----------|\n| **Mississippi** | 248.0 | 178.4 | 34.7 |\n| **Oklahoma** | 257.1 | 171.6 | 33.8 |\n| **Arkansas** | 224.1 | 168.1 | 36.0 |\n| **West Virginia** | 209.5 | 176.3 | 41.1 |\n\n**Four states** exceeded all three thresholds in 2022.\n\n---\n\n## Verification Details\n\n**Thresholds:**\n- Heart disease > 191.5 per 100,000\n- Cancer > 161.9 per 100,000  \n- Diabetes > 33.1 per 100,000\n\n**States that met 2 of 3 (for reference):**\n- Kentucky: Heart (208.6) ✓, Cancer (177.3) ✓, Diabetes (29.4) ✗\n- Tennessee: Heart (218.3) ✓, Cancer (162.3) ✓, Diabetes (31.2) ✗\n- Louisiana: Heart (224.0) ✓, Cancer (160.3) ✗, Diabetes (29.9) ✗\n- Indiana: Heart (185.0) ✗, Cancer (162.5) ✓, Diabetes (29.8) ✗\n\n## Sources\n\n1. **Heart disease**: KFF State Health Facts – \"Heart Disease Deaths and Death Rates per 100,000 Population\" (2022), sourced from CDC WONDER: https://www.kff.org/state-health-policy-data/state-indicator/heart-disease-deaths-and-death-rates-per-100000-population\n2. **Cancer**: KFF State Health Facts – \"Cancer Deaths and Death Rates per 100,000 Population\" (2022), sourced from CDC WONDER: https://www.kff.org/state-health-policy-data/state-indicator/cancer-deaths-and-death-rates-per-100000\n3. **Diabetes**: KFF State Health Facts – \"Diabetes Deaths and Death Rates per 100,000 Population\" (2022), sourced from CDC WONDER: https://www.kff.org/state-health-policy-data/state-indicator/diabetes-deaths-and-death-rates-per-100000\n\nAll rates are age-adjusted to the 2000 US standard population per 100,000.",
